        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(memory-lancedb): gate auto-recall/auto-capture on per-agent memorySearch.enabled (#103590)",
        "body": "## Summary\n\nFixes #103590.\n\n`memory-lancedb`'s `before_prompt_build` auto-recall hook only checked the plugin-level `autoRecall` flag. Agents configured with `memorySearch.enabled: false` still received `<relevant-memories>` blocks injected from the shared LanceDB store — silently leaking one agent's private memories (tracking numbers, personal info) into every other agent's prompts. The `agent_end` auto-capture path had the sibling gap: a memory-disabled agent's conversations were still written into the shared store other agents recall from.\n\n### Change\n\n`extensions/memory-lancedb/index.ts`:\n- New `agentMemorySearchEnabled(agentId)` helper reading the live runtime config: per-agent `agents.list[].memorySearch.enabled` wins over `agents.defaults.memorySearch.enabled`; unset means enabled — mirroring core `resolveMemorySearchConfig` gating semantics (`src/agents/memory-search.ts`). Plain config reads through the plugin's existing `api.runtime.config.current()` access; no new SDK surface.\n- `before_prompt_build` (auto-recall injection) and `agent_end` (auto-capture) both gate on it via the hook's `ctx.agentId`. When runtime config is unavailable (startup-config-only harnesses), behavior is unchanged.\n- `docs/plugins/memory-lancedb.md`: documents the per-agent gate.\n\nThis is the issue's Option A (respect `memorySearch.enabled`) — the minimal authoritative gate, extended to the capture sibling so a disabled agent neither reads from nor writes to the shared store. Per-agent database partitioning (Option C) is a larger product decision left to maintainers.\n\n## Verification\n\n- New regression `gates auto-recall and auto-capture on the agent's memorySearch.enabled` — verified failing without the fix (recall ran for the disabled agent) and passing with it. Covers: disabled agent (no embed call, no store write), enabled agent (recall + capture proceed), and defaults-level disable for unlisted agents.\n- `pnpm test extensions/memory-lancedb` — 2 files, 136 tests passed.\n- `pnpm tsgo:extensions`, `pnpm tsgo:extensions:test`, oxfmt, oxlint — clean.\n\n## Real behavior proof\n\nBehavior addressed: cross-agent memory leakage — `<relevant-memories>` injected into agents with `memorySearch.enabled: false`, and their conversations captured into the shared store.\nReal environment tested: the plugin's real `register()` and hook handlers driven through the existing memory-lancedb test harness with a live runtime config mirroring the issue's exact two-agent repro config (`main` enabled, `xiaohuo` disabled) and mocked LanceDB/embeddings at the module boundary.\nExact steps or command run after this patch: `pnpm test extensions/memory-lancedb`\nEvidence after fix: for `ctx.agentId: \"xiaohuo\"` the recall hook returns undefined with zero embedding calls and zero store writes; for `\"main\"` recall embeds the query and capture stores the memory; with `agents.defaults.memorySearch.enabled: false` an unlisted agent is also skipped.\nObserved result after fix: 136/136 tests pass; before the fix the new test fails because recall executes for the disabled agent.\nWhat was not tested: a live gateway run with a real Ollama/LanceDB stack; the gate sits before any embedding/store I/O so the harness boundary covers the decision exactly.\n",
        "branch": "fix/103590-lancedb-per-agent-memory-gate",
